  id: 'purusha',
  about: 'This is one of the most important and most argued-over hymns in the Rig Veda — sixteen verses that imagine the entire universe as a single vast Person, Puruṣa, from whose sacrifice everything is made. Its central vision is staggering and, in one sense, radically unifying: the sun, the moon, the sky, the animals, the very hymns of the Veda, and all human beings are limbs of one cosmic body. But this same hymn also contains a single verse — the twelfth — that would become the most consequential sentence in Indian social history: the first text ever to name the four social classes and locate them in parts of that body. We include Puruṣa because it is impossible to understand the Vedas honestly and leave it out, and because honesty is precisely the point. Below you will find the whole hymn, and where the twelfth verse comes, a plain account of what it says, what scholars believe about when it was added, and the tension it holds with the hymn\'s own vision of unity. No mystification, and no looking away.',
