      commentary: 'Now the hymn turns to its strangest and most profound conception: creation as a sacrifice in which the Person is himself the offering. The gods perform a rite — but there is nothing yet in existence to offer, so they offer the Person, the All, and from that self-giving everything is made. Notice the seasons standing in for the ritual materials: spring is the melted butter, summer the fuel, autumn the oblation. Time itself is part of the ceremony. The idea beneath the imagery is one of the deepest in the Veda: that existence comes into being through an act of self-offering — the whole gives itself up, is divided and distributed, so that the many can be. Creation here is not a command or a craftsmanship but a sacrifice, a giving-away. The world is what the Person became by letting himself be shared out.'
