      transliteration: 'yajñena yajñamayajanta devāstāni dharmāṇi prathamānyāsan\nte ha nākaṁ mahimānaḥ sacanta yatra pūrve sādhyāḥ santi devāḥ',
      translation: 'With the sacrifice the gods sacrificed to the sacrifice; these were the first ordinances. Those mighty ones reached the height of heaven, where the ancient gods, the Sadhyas, dwell.',
      commentary: 'The final verse folds back on itself in a line that has puzzled and delighted readers for three thousand years: yajñena yajñam ayajanta devāḥ — "with the sacrifice the gods sacrificed to the sacrifice." The offering, the act of offering, and the one to whom it is offered are all the same thing — the Person, who is at once victim, rite, and god. It is the hymn\'s deepest and most vertiginous idea: at the root of things is a self-referring act of self-giving, reality offering itself to itself in order to become. And these, the verse says, "were the first ordinances," tāni dharmāṇi prathamāni — the first dharmas, the earliest patterns that everything after would follow. The word dharma, which will become the great keyword of the Gita and of all Indian ethics, makes one of its first appearances here, meaning the primal template of how things are done. The hymn ends by saying: this self-sacrifice was the first law, the pattern beneath all patterns — and everything that gives itself away so that something else may live is, faintly, repeating it.'
